    "content": "Somali Defense Minister Ahmed Moalim Fiqi headed a delegation that participated in the opening ceremony of the 13th Beijing Xiangshan Forum in Beijing, China, on September 15, 2026. The forum, scheduled to run until September 17, brings together defense leaders, officials, experts, and representatives from nearly 100 countries and international organizations.\n\nThe Xiangshan Forum is a prominent platform for dialogue on global security issues, hosted annually by China. This year's theme is \"Building Consensus on Stability and Jointly Promoting Security Governance.\" The agenda includes discussions on regional stability, counterterrorism, and maritime security, among other topics.\n\nMinister Fiqi's attendance underscores Somalia's engagement with international partners on security matters. The Somali government has been actively participating in global forums to bolster its efforts in stabilizing the country and combating militant groups. Somalia has previously used such platforms to seek support for its security sector reforms and to highlight its progress in rebuilding state institutions.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this participation signals the federal government's commitment to international cooperation, which is crucial for the country's stability and development. Many diaspora members closely follow Somalia's security and political developments, as they often contribute to reconstruction efforts and maintain familial ties. Engagement with major powers like China can also open avenues for economic and diplomatic support that benefit the diaspora through improved investment and travel opportunities.\n\nThe conference provides an opportunity for Somalia to strengthen bilateral ties with China and other nations. China has been involved in various infrastructure and development projects in Somalia and has supported African Union missions in the region. Defense cooperation could further enhance Somalia's capacity to address security challenges.\n\nAs the forum continues, discussions are expected to focus on collaborative approaches to security governance. Somalia's participation reflects its ongoing efforts to integrate into the global security architecture and to learn from the experiences of other nations. The outcome of these talks may influence future security strategies and partnerships in the Horn of Africa.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/16/wasiirka-gaashaandhigga-soomaaliya-oo-ka-qaybgalay-madasha-13-aad-ee-beijing-xiangshan/",
