    "summary": "The National Independent Electoral Commission met with Women and Family Affairs Minister Khadija Al-Makhsuumi to review progress on preserving the 30% women's quota and ensuring participation of people with special needs in elections.",
    "content": "The National Independent Electoral Commission and the Ministry of Women and Family Affairs held a meeting on Tuesday at the commission's headquarters in Mogadishu. The discussions focused on women's participation in elections, particularly safeguarding the 30 percent quota for women, and on ensuring that people with special needs can take part in the electoral process.\n\nThe meeting was led by the Minister of Women and Family Affairs, Khadija Al-Makhsuumi, along with senior officials from her ministry. It followed earlier consultations between the two bodies, during which they reviewed progress made in protecting the 30 percent quota. The commission and the ministry have been working together to address barriers that hinder women's political representation.\n\nThe 30 percent quota for women in Somalia's federal parliament was established as part of efforts to increase female participation in politics. However, its implementation has faced challenges, including clan-based selection processes and cultural norms. The commission and the ministry have previously held talks on how to enforce the quota in upcoming elections.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this development is significant because many diaspora Somalis advocate for inclusive governance and support relatives back home. The diaspora often funds and monitors electoral processes, and a strengthened women's quota could lead to more representative decision-making that reflects the interests of all Somalis, including those abroad. Additionally, ensuring accessibility for people with special needs aligns with diaspora calls for a fair and transparent electoral system.\n\nThe meeting concluded with both sides reaffirming their commitment to continue cooperation on these issues. No specific new measures were announced, but the dialogue signals ongoing efforts to make Somalia's elections more inclusive. The international community, including diaspora organizations, has consistently urged Somali authorities to uphold the 30 percent quota and protect the rights of marginalized groups.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/16/guddiga-doorashooyinka-iyo-wasaaradda-haweenka-oo-ka-wada-hadlay-qoondada-haweenka/",
